--!strict
-- Things that happen while you are working. Delivered by the "Shift" route,
-- which means they are fired by WorkService partway through a shift and can
-- never be handed out by a birthday or left on the doormat -- a customer cannot
-- be shouting at you in your own kitchen.
--
-- Written to be true of any job wherever possible, because there are two and
-- neither is special enough to need its own pool. The office events are gated by
-- age rather than by job for the same reason: at eighteen the office is the job
-- you have, so age is doing the work already and a second mechanism would be
-- machinery with nothing to hold.
--
-- These are answered by walking where the room allows it. A shift is the least
-- interesting four minutes in the game if nothing happens in it, and every one of
-- these is a small thing that costs something -- an hour, twenty dollars, the
-- rest of your evening, being the person who said it out loud.
--
-- Two fields carry the weight here.
--
-- `performance` is what the answer does to your record at the job you are working,
-- and it is required on every choice in this file. The interesting ones are where
-- it disagrees with the stats: telling a rude customer exactly what you think is
-- worth real happiness and real damage to your standing, and both of those are
-- true. An answer that is good for you and bad for your record is the most
-- honest thing this system can offer, and there should always be some.
--
-- `repeatable` marks the events that are texture rather than milestones. A quiet
-- hour with nobody in the shop happens a hundred times in a working life; being
-- handed the keys happens once. Selection always prefers something unlived, so
-- marking an event repeatable never costs a fresh one its turn -- it only means
-- that a career longer than the content does not fall silent.

